   "text": "This SMILES was read from the drawing, and two parts are uncertain: the length of the diacid chain and some of the wedge/hash readings.\n\n**Reading from the C-terminus (top left) to the N-terminus (bottom):**\n1. A primary amide \u2013C(=O)NH2 on N-methylglycine (sarcosine).\n2. 3-pyridylalanine.\n3. Asn.\n4. Lys with an N(Me)Ac side chain.\n5. 4-amino-tetrahydropyran-4-carboxylic acid (achiral).\n6. 2-naphthylalanine.\n7. Tyr with an O-CH2CH2NH2 ether.\n8. Penicillamine (Pen1), one half of the disulfide.\n9. Lys with an acetylated \u03b5-amine.\n10. 7-methyltryptophan.\n11. Thr.\n12. Asn.\n13. Penicillamine (Pen2), joined to Pen1 by the S\u2013S bond to close the macrocycle.\n14. The N-terminal Lys, which carries two branches:\n   - Its \u03b1-NH is acylated by two 8-amino-3,6-dioxaoctanoyl (OEG) units, then \u03b3-Glu, then a C20 diacid chain ending in COOH.\n   - Its \u03b5-NH is acylated by a succinyl group linked to 3-amino-4-(trimethylammonio)butanoic acid.\n\n**Stereochemistry from wedges and hashes:**\n- Residues 2\u20134, 6\u201312, the Pen1/Pen2 centres and the \u03b3-Glu read as L.\n- The second Asn (12) and the N-terminal Lys (14) read as D, because their wedge bonds run clockwise.\n- The Thr \u03b2-carbon reads as R.\n- The trimethylammonio acid reads as R.\n\n**Uncertainties:**\n- The diacid chain is hard to count in the image. I took it as 20 carbons (an icosanedioic acid acyl), but 18 is also plausible.\n- Some hash bonds were hard to resolve, so those residues are assigned L by default.\n\nSMILES: NC(=O)CN(C)C(=O)[C@H](Cc1cccnc1)NC(=O)[C@H](CC(N)=O)NC(=O)[C@H](CCCCN(C)C(C)=O)NC(=O)C1(CCOCC1)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc1ccc2ccccc2c1)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc1ccc(OCCN)cc1)NC(=O)[C@H](C(C)(C)S%10)NC(=O)[C@H](CCCCNC(C)=O)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc2c[nH]c3c(C)cccc23)NC(=O)[C@H]([C@@H](C)O)NC(=O)[C@@H](CC(N)=O)NC(=O)[C@H](C(C)(C)S%10)NC(=O)[C@@H](CCCCNC(=O)CCC(=O)N[C@H](CC(=O)O)C[N+](C)(C)C)NC(=O)COCCOCCNC(=O)COCCOCCNC(=O)CC[C@H](NC(=O)CCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCC(=O)O)C(=O)O"
